+{"created_at": "2026-06-29T21:15:28.640711+00:00", "ocr_backend": "sglang", "ocr_endpoint_version": "modal-sglang-unlimited-ocr-v1", "ocr_model": "Unlimited-OCR", "ocr_model_version": "Unlimited-OCR", "ocr_text": "<PAGE><|det|>table [84, 58, 905, 130]<|/det|><table><tr><td>M-2</td><td colspan=\"3\">Incorrect Balance Increase Calculation Due to Validators&#x27; Exit</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Medium</td><td>Status</td><td>Acknowledged</td></tr></table>\n<|det|>title [81, 149, 187, 162]<|/det|>Description\n<|det|>text [80, 166, 860, 197]<|/det|>This issue has been identified within the _checkAnnualBalancesIncrease function of the OracleReportSanityChecker contract.\n<|det|>text [80, 200, 896, 318]<|/det|>The _preCLBalance parameter does not account for the withdrawal vault balance, yet it includes new validators. It is possible for N new validators to enter the protocol while simultaneously N old validators exit the protocol. In such a scenario, the balanceIncrease OracleReportSanityChecker.sol#L710 than the actual growth of the total consensus layer balance. As a result, the subsequent check will fail to detect anomalous balance growth. Also, OracleReportSanityChecker.sol#L704 may pass and return early without any checks for balance increase.\n<|det|>text [80, 321, 886, 355]<|/det|>The issue is classified as Medium severity because it undermines the effectiveness of an important security control designed to detect abnormal consensus layer balance increases.\n<|det|>title [81, 374, 214, 386]<|/det|>Recommendation\n<|det|>text [80, 390, 905, 422]<|/det|>We recommend modifying the balance increase calculation to properly account for validators' exit.\n<|det|>title [81, 444, 234, 457]<|/det|>Client's Commentary:\n<|det|>text [81, 460, 460, 474]<|/det|>Client: https://github.com/lidofinance/core/issues/1372\n<|det|>text [81, 478, 152, 491]<|/det|>Example?\n<|det|>text [80, 495, 831, 526]<|/det|>MixBytes:_preCLBalance = _pre.clBalance + (_post.clValidators - _pre.clValidators) * DEPOSIT_SIZE\n<|det|>text [80, 530, 915, 596]<|/det|>If there were some deposits on validators between the 2 reports (let's say we deposited 100 new validators) and some of the withdrawals were finalized between the 2 reports (let's say 20 validators were finalized and their principal balance was sent to EL) the _preCLBalance will account only for 100 new validators (since the withdrawal vault balance is not accounted here), but the _postCLBalance will increase only by 80 validators (100 - 20).\n<|det|>text [80, 600, 906, 649]<|/det|>Client: There is no simple way to track validator exits in accounting. If we simply add the balance of the withdraw vault, that opens a DoS vector on the oracle report by allowing anyone to inflate the withdraw vault balance from the outside. It's not expensive to execute, but it's available to anyone.\n<|det|>text [80, 652, 898, 684]<|/det|>It looks like the current approach is optimal from the sanity-checks perspective. We can update the docs and state that we limit growth of the aggregate balance (excluding deposits) over a period to prevent the misunderstanding in the future.\n<|det|>text [80, 687, 852, 719]<|/det|>MixBytes: We would also recommend emitting an event inside the if (_preCLBalance >= _postCLBalance) return; branch of the _checkAnnualBalancesIncrease function to track such cases in the off-chain logic.\n<|det|>footer [82, 949, 248, 972]<|/det|>MixBytes()\n<|det|>page_number [880, 951, 917, 970]<|/det|>17\n", "page_image_sha256": "08307f4b7f02ca6c4d9d949ca0b3dc8bd045ca53a24a6d8563f28d26869cf1c9", "page_number": 18, "pdf_id": "pdf_003436_1e68fc3994b5", "raw_record": {"code_blocks": [], "confidence": null, "created_at": "2026-06-29T21:15:28.640711+00:00", "layout_blocks": [], "ocr_backend": "sglang", "ocr_endpoint_version": "modal-sglang-unlimited-ocr-v1", "ocr_model": "Unlimited-OCR", "ocr_model_version": "Unlimited-OCR", "ocr_settings": {"dpi": 300, "image_mode": "base", "mode": "pages", "ngram_size": 35, 